{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:20660314 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:20660314 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"20660314","source":"MED","pmid":"20660314","pmcid":"PMC2922514","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2922514"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.1005399107","title":"Bacterial phenylalanine and phenylacetate catabolic pathway revealed.","authorString":"Teufel R, Mascaraque V, Ismail W, Voss M, Perera J, Eisenreich W, Haehnel W, Fuchs G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Teufel R","lastName":"Teufel","firstName":"R","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5863-7248"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mikrobiologie, Fakultät für Biologie, Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg, D-79104 Freiburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Mascaraque V","lastName":"Mascaraque","firstName":"V","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1139-0553"}},{"fullName":"Ismail W","lastName":"Ismail","firstName":"W","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Voss M","lastName":"Voss","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Perera J","lastName":"Perera","firstName":"J","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Eisenreich W","lastName":"Eisenreich","firstName":"W","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Haehnel W","lastName":"Haehnel","firstName":"W","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Fuchs G","lastName":"Fuchs","firstName":"G","initials":"G"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5863-7248"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3027-928X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1139-0553"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"32","volume":"107","journalIssueId":1753766,"dateOfPublication":"2010 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2010,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","nlmid":"7505876"},"printPublicationDate":"2010-08-01"},"pubYear":"2010","pageInfo":"14390-14395","abstractText":"Aromatic compounds constitute the second most abundant class of organic substrates and environmental pollutants, a substantial part of which (e.g., phenylalanine or styrene) is metabolized by bacteria via phenylacetate. Surprisingly, the bacterial catabolism of phenylalanine and phenylacetate remained an unsolved problem. Although a phenylacetate metabolic gene cluster had been identified, the underlying biochemistry remained largely unknown. Here we elucidate the catabolic pathway functioning in 16% of all bacteria whose genome has been sequenced, including Escherichia coli and Pseudomonas putida. This strategy is exceptional in several aspects. Intermediates are processed as CoA thioesters, and the aromatic ring of phenylacetyl-CoA becomes activated to a ring 1,2-epoxide by a distinct multicomponent oxygenase. The reactive nonaromatic epoxide is isomerized to a seven-member O-heterocyclic enol ether, an oxepin. This isomerization is followed by hydrolytic ring cleavage and beta-oxidation steps, leading to acetyl-CoA and succinyl-CoA. This widespread paradigm differs significantly from the established chemistry of aerobic aromatic catabolism, thus widening our view of how organisms exploit such inert substrates. It provides insight into the natural remediation of man-made environmental contaminants such as styrene. Furthermore, this pathway occurs in various pathogens, where its reactive early intermediates may contribute to virulence.","affiliation":"Department of Mikrobiologie, Fakultät für Biologie, Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg, D-79104 Freiburg, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bacteria","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pseudomonas putida","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Escherichia coli","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Phenylacetates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Styrene","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Phenylalanine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome, Bacterial"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Multigene Family"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Biodegradation, Environmental"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Metabolic Networks and Pathways","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Phenylacetates","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Phenylalanine","registryNumber":"47E5O17Y3R"},{"name":"Styrene","registryNumber":"44LJ2U959V"},{"name":"phenylacetic acid","registryNumber":"ER5I1W795A"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free after 6 months","availabilityCode":"F6","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"www.pnas.org","url":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/reprint/107/32/14390.pdf"},